# THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR THE COMPOSE STACK'S OWN CONFIGURATION.
#
# The sibling of backend/.env.example, and it works the same way: the deploy
# workflow reads the keys below, resolves each one from GitHub Variables or
# Secrets, and FAILS THE BUILD if a required value is missing or empty. It is
# rendered by deploy/render-env.sh and lands on the box as ~/stack/.env.
#
# It replaces a fixed heredoc in the workflows -- the same pattern, and for the
# same reason, that backend/.env.example already replaced. That heredoc is how
# CRM_DB_USER reached the test box as an empty string: vars.CRM_DB_USER_TEST was
# never set in GitHub, the heredoc wrote `CRM_DB_USER=` without complaint, and
# nothing anywhere reported it. It did no damage only because the volume was
# already initialised, so the init script that reads it never ran again. At prod
# cutover -- the single shot that script gets -- it would have created the CRM
# role as crm/crm instead.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. A line ending in "# optional" may be empty in
# a deployed environment; everything else must have a value.

# --- Compose's own settings -----------------------------------------------
#
# On the box this file is named .env, which is the file Compose reads by
# default, and COMPOSE_FILE is what points it at the deployed compose file.
# Together they are what make a bare `docker compose up -d`, `ps` or `logs`
# work in ~/stack -- no -f, no --env-file, nothing to remember. deploy.sh runs
# the identical bare command, so the deploy path and the debugging path cannot
# drift apart.
#
# DO NOT move these variables into a separate file and point at it with
# COMPOSE_ENV_FILES. That variable is only honoured from the real environment;
# set inside .env it is ignored, and Compose then interpolates every value
# below to a blank string with a warning rather than an error. Setting it in
# the environment instead drops .env altogether, losing COMPOSE_FILE with it.
# Both were tested against the Compose v5.5.1 on these boxes.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.prod.yml

# --- Postgres -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# POSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_PASSWORD are READ EXACTLY ONCE, by the Postgres
# entrypoint, on the first `up` against an empty volume. On a volume that
# already holds data they are ignored completely -- changing them later does
# nothing at all, and correcting one then means ALTER ROLE by hand.
#
# The role carries the environment (ticketing_dev / ticketing_test /
# ticketing_prod) while the database is ticketing_saas everywhere, because
# isolation here is per box: the suffix is what makes a credential obviously
# one environment's when it shows up in a log or a config file.
#
# backend.env's DATABASE_URL is built from these two in the workflow rather
# than stored as a URL of its own, so the role and password the app connects
# with cannot drift from the ones the database was created with.
#
# THE PASSWORD IS SPLICED INTO THAT URL, so keep it to characters that carry no
# meaning in one: A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ ~ - are all safe. A `@`, `:`, `/`, `?`, `#` or
# `%` would silently cut the URL in the wrong place and the app would fail to
# connect with an error naming a host or database nobody configured. The values
# in use as of 2026-09-07 were checked and are safe; this is a constraint on the
# next rotation, not an outstanding problem.
POSTGRES_USER=ticketing_dev
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=ticketing_dev

# Same one-shot rule, same convention -- see postgres-init/01-create-crm-database.sh.
CRM_DB_USER=crm_dev
CRM_DB_PASSWORD=crm_dev

# --- Images ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Supplied by the build job, not by GitHub config: both are pinned to the
# commit SHA that CI actually built and tested. This box pulls and never
# builds.
BACKEND_IMAGE=ghcr.io/anyth3ng/ticketing-saas-backend:latest
FRONTEND_IMAGE=ghcr.io/anyth3ng/ticketing-saas-frontend:latest
